@* A heading that may name a class.

   Six of the catalogue's headings are a bare class name — `.card-grid`,
   `.md-editor` — and one contains a tag name. They read as code and should look
   like it, but Title has to stay a plain string: it is the anchor slug, the
   accessible name, and what the MCP server returns.

   So backticks mark code, exactly as they would in the prose around them. Blazor
   escapes the text nodes, which is what makes `<dialog>` safe to write. *@
@foreach (var (text, isCode) in Parts)
{
    if (isCode)
    {
        <code>@text</code>
    }
    else
    {
        @text
    }
}

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Text { get; set; } = default!;

    /// The title with the backticks removed — for a slug, an aria-label, or an index.
    public static string Plain(string text) =>
        text.Replace("`", string.Empty, StringComparison.Ordinal);

    private IEnumerable<(string Text, bool IsCode)> Parts =>
        // Odd segments are inside a backtick pair. An unclosed backtick leaves its
        // tail as the last segment, so it renders as code rather than vanishing.
        Text.Split('`').Select((part, i) => (part, i % 2 == 1)).Where(p => p.part.Length > 0);
}
